WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.430
(dramatic music)

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.740
- Being in a shipyard in general sucks

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.683
without any of the COVID stuff.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:10.710
- [Narrator] Madeleine Wolczko works

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.120
on an American container ship

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.860
that got stuck in a Shanghai shipyard

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.630
during the city's COVID lockdown

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.540
and she documented her
two-months-long experience.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:19.860
- Once the lockdown happened,

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.670
it was just kind of one
thing after the next.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.050
- [Narrator] She and the crew had to rely

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.060
on local authorities for supplies.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.060
- [Madeleine] We have a
delivery here of food and water.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.910
- [Narrator] Took regular COVID tests

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.932
all while living on a
semi-dismantled ship.

00:00:35.932 --> 00:00:37.182
- The rainforest is back.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.730
We're just trying to keep
busy just to stay sane.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.710
- [Narrator] Their struggle
shows just how hard it was

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.170
for shipyards, as well as
companies in the region

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.903
to keep working through
Shanghai's COVID restrictions.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.530
In February, the President Wilson docked

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.690
at a shipyard just
north of the city's port

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.340
for repairs that were originally meant

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.470
to take a little over a month.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.990
- The ship works 24/7.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:02.190
We don't stop.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.110
- [Narrator] Wolczko is
the ship's second officer

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.580
and is from Washington State.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.350
She gave The Wall Street
Journal permission

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:08.583
to use her footage.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.340
The plan was to finish the maintenance,

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.230
then pick up containers at the port.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:14.940
Wolczko says when the works started,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.160
some days about 500 shipyard workers

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.450
would come aboard.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:19.830
But about a month into their stay,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.660
Shanghai entered a lockdown.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.370
Wolczko says the workers stopped coming

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.860
and most of the repair
work ground to a halt.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.370
- We're kind of already
used to being onboard,

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.740
the difference being
that we're not moving,

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:33.723
there's no work progressing.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.440
- [Narrator] Restricted to
the ship by local authorities,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.940
the crew was stuck on a vessel

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.470
that was midway through repairs.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.217
- [Madeleine] It's starting to
get flooded already in here.

00:01:43.217 --> 00:01:44.430
- [Narrator] Unable to stock up

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:45.840
on their regular food supplies,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.970
they relied on government deliveries.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:49.260
- At the beginning of the lockdown,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.990
we had about a month's meat left.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:55.800
It was a little terrifying.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.270
- [Narrator] The crew also had to undergo

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.740
regular COVID testing,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.980
following a schedule set by
the shipyard authorities.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.000
- The PCR test shall be done

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.048
on the first, second,
fourth, seventh, 10th,

00:02:05.048 --> 00:02:08.010
14th, 17th and 21st days.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:09.750
- [Narrator] Wolczko says
all of this took a toll

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.820
on the mental health of the crew.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:16.800
- It's difficult when there's no set date,

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.750
no set plan.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.030
Everyone's up and down all the time.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.190
- [Narrator] The company CMA CGM Group,

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:24.720
which owns the President Wilson

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.580
said they were in daily
contact with the crew

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.410
to ensure their health and safety,

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.790
and worked with local authorities

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.923
to organize food and other necessities.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.610
About a month after things came to a halt,

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.140
the shipyard implemented a system

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.360
that would allow workers
to return to the vessel,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.760
despite the city's COVID restrictions.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.380
It's called a closed loop.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.810
- Basically, the workers are locked down

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.560
in a confined area

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.550
and they're not allowed to leave

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.440
but they can still work.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.420
- [Narrator] This closed off
environment wasn't only used

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.580
at the shipyard but it's
also been implemented

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.770
by factories and financial companies

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.170
where employees lived and slept at work.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.870
Some even setting up tents for privacy.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:04.980
It's a way for officials

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.930
to balance two competing goals:

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.400
curb the spread of COVID,

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.920
and keep production going
to limit economic damage

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.170
and global supply chain fallout.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:14.250
Companies like Tesla

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.100
and China's largest
state-owned automaker SAIC

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.450
all implemented closed loops

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:19.893
during Shanghai's lockdown.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.150
But this system isn't always
running at 100% capacity

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:25.650
compared to normal times.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.630
- Closed loop does not mean perfect,

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:28.890
it doesn't mean perfect loop.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.120
- [Narrator] Jarrod Ward is the chairman

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.260
of the supply chain committee

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.870
at the American Chamber
of Commerce in Shanghai.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.030
He also works for Yusen Logistics,

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.460
a company that organizes
and manages the transport

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.220
of goods at the port for
suppliers and retailers.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.470
- You're basically concentrating the labor

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.390
without allowing them to go home.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.400
I mean, there's a human toll to that,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.740
as well as just the
general lack of efficiency

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.320
of having enough people.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.640
- [Narrator] Many of the city's shipyards

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.590
also faced reduced capacity.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.630
According to the President Wilson's crew,

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.030
only a fraction of the
original workforce returned

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.890
to the ship when work resumed.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:03.150
- And it was just a trickle,

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.800
like it was 10 people,

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:05.760
and then it was 20 people,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.860
and then it was 50 to 60 people.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:08.910
- [Narrator] Along with fewer people,

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.530
there were not enough materials coming

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.080
into shipyards in Shanghai for repairs.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.960
That's because the lockdown
created a logistics headache.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.550
Truck drivers coming into the city

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.680
had to take COVID tests or quarantine,

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.230
slowing down deliveries
to factories and the port.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.750
- Even though you may have
the closed loop labor on site,

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.000
you still don't have your components

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.770
flowing into the manufacturing site.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.660
- [Narrator] So with fewer
people and materials,

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.940
the ship's repairs dragged on for months,

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:36.270
and the President Wilson's
crew simply had to wait.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.280
- We're never gonna get out of here.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:39.240
- [Narrator] The Port of Shanghai

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.890
and the company that owns the shipyard,

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.780
COSCO SHIPPING Heavy Industry,

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.120
didn't respond to requests for comment.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.190
The city's government
lifted COVID restrictions

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:49.751
on June 1st, and about a week later,

00:04:49.751 --> 00:04:50.610
(drilling sounds)

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:52.620
the President Wilson completed its repairs

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.020
and set out to pick up cargo at the port.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.550
- We are finally on our
way down the Yangtze River.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:01.230
- [Narrator] But there's still
uncertainty for companies

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:03.090
that rely on shipping and manufacturing

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:04.020
in the region.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.120
- The biggest concern
is this COVID situation

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:07.920
we went through in Shanghai

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.480
easily could come back next month

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.880
or it could come back in another location.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.040
And how is your supply chain designed?

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.070
Is it flexible enough to
actually deal with that?

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.120
- [Narrator] Shipping analysts say

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.970
as trucking and activity at
the port return to normal,

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.010
they expect the resumption
of export shipments

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:24.480
to have a ripple effect,

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.570
creating congestion at other
major ports around the world,

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:29.280
especially as the back to school

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.920
and holiday shopping
seasons start to kick in.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.300
Companies operating in China

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.830
could become increasingly wary

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.770
of more disruptions caused
by strict COVID strategies.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.810
- That's why we see people deciding

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:41.490
about alternative supply points,

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:44.070
whether they're still
inside of China or outside.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.390
So this is where the impact

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:48.600
will be felt for I think
many, certainly months,

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.043
but many years to come.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:51.833
- [Narrator] The President Wilson

00:05:51.833 --> 00:05:54.480
is finding its way back
into the global supply chain

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:55.800
with its final destination

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.140
at the US's busiest container port complex

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.570
at Los Angeles and Long Beach.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.690
- People don't really think
about the humans involved

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.430
and that we have limits.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:06.780
Just go, go, go all the time.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.550
Think about the cargo,
and think about the money,

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:09.540
and think about the ship.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:11.940
When you're already fatigued,

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:16.653
sleep deprived, it's
excruciating at times.

